WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.560
This year, we've lost actors who flitted between
movies and television over the course of their

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.440
careers, while some made their biggest impact
on Broadway.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.660
What they all have in common is a dedication
to their craft and a fanbase that will miss

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.660
them dearly.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.220
You might remember him as Murray Futterman
from the '80s classic Gremlins, but the man

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.560
behind the paranoid WWII vet was prolific
screen actor Dick Miller.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.860
The Bronx-born actor made quite the name for
himself in the boxing ring while serving in

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:32.259
the U.S. Navy, but his career path swerved
when he met legendary B-movie producer Roger

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.860
Corman in the 1950s.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.080
He starred in a number of Corman's movies,
but Miller's biggest role came towards the

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.670
end of the decade, when he starred as murderous
busboy Walter Paisley in 1959's A Bucket of

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:44.400
Blood.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.180
After Variety announced Miller's death in
January, Joe Dante, who directed Miller in

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.460
Gremlins among other films, tweeted a tribute
to Miller.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.700
The director wrote,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:11.640
Steven Levy, known professionally as Steve
Bean, sadly succumbed to a rare form of nose

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.060
cancer in January 2019.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.539
The comedian and actor went into great detail
about his shock 2016 diagnosis in an essay

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:25.020
titled "My Year Without a Nose," published
by Mel magazine in 2018, revealing that he'd

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.120
been given a year to live after undergoing
surgery to remove his nose.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.820
Levy managed to fight on for a while, passing
away towards the end of January.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.040
He was 58.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.840
According to Variety, the Massachusetts-native
got his start on the stand-up scene, becoming

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.360
a household name in Boston.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.939
He later moved to Los Angeles and started
writing for the Tim Conway Show and ABC's

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.460
Dot Comedy, which gave him a foot in the door.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.660
He scored minor parts in shows like Quantum
Leap and Murder, She Wrote, carving out a

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.100
career as a dependable television actor.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.500
He worked right up until his diagnosis.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.480
Levy is survived by his wife, Caroline Carrigan,
and his son, Jacob Randall Levy.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.780
Sci-fi fans will no doubt recognize him as
guest-star-turned-regular Jacob Carter from

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.520
Stargate SG-1, but Carmen Argenziano played
a variety of roles throughout a long career

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:23.700
in the business.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.500
He got his start in the early '70s and eventually
settled into a career as a character actor

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.120
in the decades that followed, becoming a regular
on our TV screens.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:38.280
Argenziano's agency Event Horizon Talent announced
his passing at the age of 75 on Facebook,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.840
where they called the veteran actor a "consummate
gentleman."

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:46.240
The post made special mention of Argenziano's
work on Stargate SG-1, saying the actor had

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.700
"relished" being on the show for seven years.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.520
Many will remember him as overly optimistic
stuntman Super Dave Osborne, but by the time

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:58.680
of his passing, writer and actor Bob Einstein
had become known for his role as Marty Funkhouser

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.820
on HBO's Curb Your Enthusiasm.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.360
"My nephew, who was an all star pitcher.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.640
He's up there now in a cast!

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.320
Why did you have to fight him for the pickle?"

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.860
Einstein passed away in January at the age
of 76, not long after being diagnosed with

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.820
cancer.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:21.319
Shortly after the news broke, Curb Your Enthusiasm
star and co-creator Larry David released a

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.360
statement, saying,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.900
Einstein emerged as one of the funniest writers
in Tinseltown during the early 1970s.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.030
He won his first Emmy for his early work on
The Smothers Brothers Comedy Hour and picked

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.320
up his second win for Dick Van Dyke's mid-70s
vehicle, Van Dyke and Company.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.060
He didn't have a whole bunch of feature-length
appearances to his name, his most notable

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:59.109
film role came in 2007 when he popped up as
Agent Caldwell in Ocean's 13, but Einstein's

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:04.080
pinpoint comic timing and unique, gravelly
voice made him a regular on American television.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:09.820
Countless actors have played the role of Ebenezer
Scrooge, but the Albert Finney-led Scrooge,

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:14.689
a 1970 version of A Christmas Carol, became
one of the most endearing, and is still beamed

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:17.380
into households across the UK every December.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.840
Younger film fans will probably recognize
him from Erin Brockovich and the James Bond

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.060
film Skyfall.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:27.260
By the time he appeared in the critically-acclaimed
spy flick, Finney had already fought kidney

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.420
cancer and won.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:33.760
He was diagnosed in 2007, but was given the
all-clear following some intense chemotherapy.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.380
While he was a popular actor and a critical
darling, Finney shunned the limelight.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:42.900
He turned down a knighthood from the queen
and received five Oscar nominations, four

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.920
of which were for Best Actor, but never showed
up to the ceremony once.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.740
Speaking to the Manchester Evening News in
2012, Finney said simply, "It seems silly

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.860
to go over there and beg for an award."

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:59.460
Skyfall, released in 2012, proved to be Finney's
final film role, but the actor remained with

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.380
us until February when he passed away at the
age of 82 after what his family called a "short

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.380
illness."

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.240
It seemed as though Carol Channing was going
to go on forever, but the smiley star of enduring

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.950
Broadway musical Hello Dolly! sadly died in
January 2019.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.760
She was 97 years old.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.780
The Seattle-born actress won a Tony for her
career-defining portrayal of matchmaker Dolly

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:25.540
Levi in the long-running production, which
paid tribute to her after learning of her

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:26.540
death.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.960
The current Hello Dolly! touring company said
in a statement:

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.560
She lost out to Marilyn Monroe when Hollywood
adapted her first theatrical hit, Gentlemen

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.880
Prefer Blondes, for the big screen in the
'50s, but that didn't stop Channing from trying

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.860
to crack the whole moving picture thing.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.020
The following decade, she succeeded.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.760
The actress won a Golden Globe and was nominated
for an Academy Award after a memorable performance

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.500
in 1967's Thoroughly Modern Millie.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.560
Julie Adams was one of Universal's leading
ladies during the 1950s, starring in the classic

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.980
1954 monster picture Creature from the Black
Lagoon.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:11.620
According to THR, the studio once declared
her legs, quote,

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.520
and even insured them to the
tune of $125,000, a staggering amount for

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:19.540
the day.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:25.400
In February 2019, her son, TV editor Mitchell
Danton, informed the Hollywood trade that

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.560
Adams had passed away in Los Angeles.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:28.960
She was 92.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.060
Adams was way more than just the object of
a lagoon-creature's affections.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.660
She starred opposite legends Elvis Presley,
and John Wayne, and made several forays into

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.080
television over the course of a long career.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.760
And when we say long, we mean long, she made
her first appearance in 1949 and her last,

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.340
in a short titled "The Lucky Southern Star,"
in 2018.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:53.840
Guillermo del Toro, whose Oscar-winning film
The Shape of Water was something of a spiritual

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.020
successor to Creature from the Black Lagoon,
was among the famous mourners.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:08.260
She was probably best-known for her engagement
to Office Space star Ron Livingston at one

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.340
stage, the pair having met on the set of 2000's
Beat.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:16.280
But Lisa Sheridan managed to hold her own
in the industry after they split in 2003.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:22.060
She made appearances in CSI: Miami and Without
a Trace the following year, and in 2005 joined

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:26.260
the cast of Invasion, starring in the ABC
mystery series for two seasons.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.520
She would go on to land various supporting
parts in a number of TV shows, but Invasion

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.740
turned out to be an early peak in a career
cut short.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:38.960
In February 2019, Sheridan's manager, Mitch
Clem, confirmed to People that the actress

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.540
had passed away at the age of 44.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:56.420
Sitcom veteran Katherine Helmond died in February
2019 of complications from Alzheimer's disease.

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:57.960
She was 89 years old.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.780
Her talent agency confirmed that Helmond,
whose film career started in the mid-1950s

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:06.860
with biblical epic Wine of Morning, passed
away at home in Los Angeles.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.560
Said former Who's the Boss Costar Judith Light,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.780
Many will remember Helmond from Soap, a controversial
parody of the conventional soap opera.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:30.160
She starred as naïve socialite Jessica Tate
from 1977 until 1981, when the ABC show stopped

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:31.160
airing.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.560
Helmond reunited with ABC in 1984 when she
agreed to play Mona Robinson in Who's the

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.140
Boss?, the role that she would ultimately
become best-known for.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.480
The two-time Golden Globe winner was on Who's
the Boss? for eight years, appearing in almost

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.320
200 episodes in that time.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.700
Helmond remained a sitcom stalwart in the
years that followed, popping up in Everybody

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:53.560
Loves Raymond sporadically for the better
part of a decade.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:55.560
But she was more than a TV star.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:00.260
She was a regular Terry Gilliam collaborator,
appearing in three of the Monty Python vet's

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:05.320
movies: she portrayed Mrs. Ogre in cult classic
Time Bandits, the surgery-loving mother in

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:10.750
Brazil, and she even had a role in drug-fueled
tour de force Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas,

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.020
playing the hotel clerk.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.980
Jan-Michael Vincent, Vincent was best-known
for '80s TV series Airwolf, passed away in

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.860
February 2019.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:24.020
The Denver native portrayed cello-playing
helicopter pilot Stringfellow Hawke on the

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:28.120
show, a slight diversion from the tough guys
and rebels he was used to playing.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:31.420
Away from the camera, Vincent had his troubles
with the law.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:36.590
He pleaded guilty to drunk driving in 1997
after breaking his own neck in a collision,

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:37.900
though he avoided jail.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:42.980
He wasn't so lucky in 2000, when he was sentenced
to 60 days behind bars for assaulting his

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:43.840
girlfriend.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.920
The actor reportedly struggled with alcoholism
and clearly had some demons, but Vincent still

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.140
had friends in Hollywood when he passed away.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:55.840
In a tweet, Vincent's Big Wednesday co-star
Gary Busey remembered his old friend:

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.200
He became a teen heartthrob playing Dylan
McKay on decade-defining '90s drama Beverly

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:09.180
Hills, 90210 and he'd experienced a recent
resurgence on the CW's Riverdale.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:14.400
But the continuing career of Luke Perry was
sadly cut short in March 2019.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.060
The actor was rushed to the hospital after
suffering what his rep described as a "massive

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:20.910
stroke" at his California home.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:25.630
He remained under observation for a further
five days before passing away with his family

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:26.840
by his bedside.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.580
He was 52 years old.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:33.660
Perry's Riverdale colleagues took the news
hard, but star Cole Sprouse was determined

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.080
to remember the good times.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.060
He explained to James Corden in March, 2019:

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:42.260
"Luke was one of those guys, I think, that
would much rather have us laughing and telling

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:46.500
stories about his life than lamenting it,
but he was a good man."

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.311
If you're a horror fan, chances are you'll
recognize character actor Joseph Pilato, who

00:10:51.311 --> 00:10:55.090
passed away in March 2019 at the age of 70.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:59.670
Pilato starred in a number of genre films
in the early stages of his career, which began

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:04.720
in 1978 when zombie movie master George A.
Romero gave him a minor role in Dawn of the

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:05.640
Dead.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:10.780
He would go on to work with Romero in 1981's
Knightriders, he played the disgruntled fairground

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:16.060
worker in the bizarre biker-gang caper, and
again in 1985's Day of the Dead, which marked

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.220
Pilato's most famous film appearance.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:23.580
The Pittsburgh actor played mentally unstable
antagonist Captain Rhodes in the grisly sequel,

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:27.500
turning in a memorable performance despite
being the character's polar opposite in real

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:28.380
life.

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:32.280
Pilato explained during a 2010 interview with
the Horror Channel:

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:51.640
His name will forever be associated with Romero's
cult zombie franchise, but what you probably

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:55.860
don't know about Pilato is that he almost
appeared in one of the most influential movies

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:01.490
of the 1990s; he was cast as a Dean Martin
look alike in Quentin Tarantino classic Pulp

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:05.320
Fiction, though unfortunately for him his
scene wound up getting cut from the final

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:05.980
film.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:11.360
Sadly, this is only a portion of the actors
who've passed away in 2019 so far.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.840
Here's a look at some of the other stars who
enhanced our lives before leaving us too soon.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.640
Paul Koslo

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.000
Kaye Ballard

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:20.740
Louisa Moritz

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.480
Kristoff St. John

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:23.920
Jed Allan

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.700
Denise DuBarry Hay

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.080
Mitzi Hoag

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:28.840
Bibi Andersson

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.560
Seymour Cassel

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:32.240
Richard Erdman

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.100
Isaac Kappy

